WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.210
Attention is a limited operating resource.

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:07.132
Demanding work depends on the ability to hold information in mind, notice what matters,

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:10.296
ignore distractions, and update understanding as new evidence appears.

00:00:10.296 --> 00:00:11.703
When attention is fragmented,

00:00:11.703 --> 00:00:15.570
energy drains faster because the brain has to keep rebuilding context.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.789
This is why a day full of interruptions can feel exhausting even

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:21.899
if no single task looks difficult.

00:00:21.899 --> 00:00:26.470
Cognitive load is the mental effort required to hold, process, and use information.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:27.876
Working memory has limits.

00:00:27.876 --> 00:00:30.689
Complex systems, unclear requirements, unfamiliar concepts, noisy environments,

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:32.798
and frequent interruptions all increase load.

00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:36.666
When load is high, people may miss details, make weaker tradeoffs,

00:00:36.666 --> 00:00:40.182
or postpone decisions because the work feels heavier than expected.

00:00:40.182 --> 00:00:42.643
Context switching is a common energy leak.

00:00:42.643 --> 00:00:47.214
Moving from a code review to a message thread, then to a meeting,

00:00:47.214 --> 00:00:50.378
then back to the code review, requires repeated reorientation.

00:00:50.378 --> 00:00:54.246
Each switch asks attention to reload the goal, the current state,

00:00:54.246 --> 00:00:55.652
and the next decision.

00:00:55.652 --> 00:00:57.762
The cost is not only time.

00:00:57.762 --> 00:01:02.333
It is also mental friction, error risk, and the gradual loss of depth.

00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:05.849
Demanding work should be protected from low-energy windows when possible.

00:01:05.849 --> 00:01:09.013
That does not mean every day will be ideal.

00:01:09.013 --> 00:01:11.826
It means matching task type to attention quality.

00:01:11.826 --> 00:01:14.638
Use higher-quality attention for design, troubleshooting, writing, learning,

00:01:14.638 --> 00:01:16.045
and decisions with consequences.

00:01:16.045 --> 00:01:19.209
Use lower-energy windows for routine cleanup, scheduling, simple approvals,

00:01:19.209 --> 00:01:20.264
and administrative batching.

00:01:20.264 --> 00:01:23.780
This makes energy a planning input rather than an afterthought.
